        "id": 243560,
        "name": "OpMegs",
        "username": "OpMegs",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/o/ce73a5/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2022-03-16T23:39:27.257Z",
        "cooked": "<p>So, recently, one of the things that’s come up in my games has been the “Respond in Kind” reaction from the Multiverse.</p>\n<p>The exact phrasing as presented in the book I have is</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When you are hit with an Attack at close range, the attacker also takes damage equal to their effect die.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now, on the face of it, this seems <em>very</em> powerful against a stronger opponent. Say your opponent hits you with a D10 effect die (but only rolls a 2). You take 2 damage and send 10 back their way. You can bypass the reaction by staying at range, but it seems like a brutal hard lockdown on a melee fighter.</p>\n<p>The other read I could take on it is that “whatever damage they rolled against you, you do back” which is a bit less consistently useful, but does mean that someone rolling high melee damage still risks a nasty backlash, but isn’t a case where the reaction effectively is just too risky to bother dealing with, since rolling 2 on your d10 doesn’t do plink damage AND get your face smashed in.</p>\n<p>Anyone had experience with this one in their games?</p>",
        "post_number": 1,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2022-03-16T23:40:06.236Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
